@* The probe: from a texel to a normal.
This part is the mathematical heart of the program. Given a material, a texel $(u,v)$
and the current shape and orientation of the solid, the \emph{inspector} computes
\begin{itemize}
\item the values of all maps at $(u,v)$;
\item the slope of the bump map there, and the normal $\vect n_{ts}$ that this slope
implies, in tangent space;
\item the point of the solid that carries this texel, the frame
$(\vect T,\vect B,\vect N)$ there, and the bumped normal $\vect n'$ in world space.
\end{itemize}
The GPU does the same computation for every pixel it draws; the inspector does it once,
on the CPU, and shows every intermediate result.

