@ The parameters that the user controls with sliders, and that both the inspector and
the shader must know, are gathered in one structure. The \emph{mid-level} of the
displacement is the height that leaves the surface where it is: with the default
$0.5$, darker texels push the surface in and lighter ones push it out.

<<Settings of bump and displacement>>=
struct BumpSettings {
    float strength = 4.0f;          // slope multiplier, per texel of the bump map
    float displacementScale = 0.05f;
    float displacementMid = 0.5f;   // height value that leaves the surface in place
    bool useDisplacement = true;
};

@ The answer of the probe. The world-space part is only meaningful when the texel lies
on an invertible patch of the current shape (\S\ref{inversion}).

<<What the probe reports>>=
struct HoverInfo {
    bool valid = false;
    DirectX::XMFLOAT2 uv{};
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 color{};
    float bump = 0, displacement = 0, roughness = 0;
    DirectX::XMFLOAT2 slope{};      // dh/dx, dh/dy in texels (central differences)
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 normalTangent{};
    // World-space quantities, available when the uv lands on the current shape.
    bool onSurface = false;
    DirectX::XMFLOAT3 position{}, normalGeom{}, normalBumped{}, tangent{}, bitangent{};
};

@
